it back to what really matters in a relationship.
23:59:41 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I hear why that logic feels intuitive, and I’m glad you asked — it gives me a chance to unpack why it’s actually a misunderstanding. Let's walk through it. The idea that "attraction to more genders = twice the temptation to cheat" assumes that cheating is simply a matter of opportunity and available options. But that’s not really how faithful relationships work. Here's why. ### Attraction doesn't equal temptation or action Think about it this way: a heterosexual man is attracted to women, broadly speaking. That doesn’t mean he’s tempted by every woman he meets. He still has a type, personal boundaries, standards, and most importantly, a commitment to his partner if he’s in a monogamous relationship. He's not walking around on the verge of cheating just because half the population matches his orientation. A bisexual person is in exactly the same position. They’re attracted to people of more than one gender — but that doesn’t mean they’re attracted to *more people overall* or to everyone they meet. Attraction doesn’t scale like that. A bisexual person isn’t attracted to literally all men and all women; they have preferences, types, and emotional filters just like anyone else. Their "pool" isn't doubled in any meaningful way that creates more temptation. ### The "blonde or brunette" analogy Imagine someone who finds both blonde and brunette people attractive. Do they have "twice the temptation" to cheat on their partner compared to someone who only likes blondes? Of course not. We intuitively understand that their partner’s hair color doesn’t determine their faithfulness. Their character and the state of their relationship do. Bisexuality is similar. It just means the gender of the person they’re attracted to isn't restricted to one — but their capacity for commitment, honesty, and respect isn’t diminished by that. Those qualities exist entirely separate from orientation. ### The real root of cheating Infidelity isn’t caused by how many genders someone is attracted to. It’s driven by personal values, emotional maturity, communication issues, relationship dissatisfaction, impulse control, or sometimes just a lack of integrity. A deeply committed, loyal bisexual person isn’t “closer” to cheating than a straight person. And a dishonest straight person isn’t magically faithful just because they’re only attracted to one gender. In fact, many bisexual people are acutely aware of this stereotype — that they’re inherently less trustworthy — and often go out of their way to be extra communicative and clear in their relationships to counteract unfair suspicion. ### A reframe So instead of “twice the options,” think of it as: this is a person who chose your daughter. Out of all the people they *could* be attracted to — regardless of gender — they are actively choosing to be with her. That’s not a threat; that’s a sign of genuine desire and commitment. Your concern comes from a protective place, and that’s admirable. But the real question you want to ask isn’t about the gender of her partner’s other potential attractions — it’s “Does this person treat her with kindness, honesty, and respect?” That will tell you infinitely more than their orientation ever could.
