heat (just like the many straight people who cheat) end up being talked about, and the stereotype feeds itself. - **My role here**: I’m not a person navigating social politeness. I’m here to be honest and clear, even when it’s uncomfortable, because you came to me for genuine advice on how to talk to your daughter. If I simply agreed with the stereotype to make you feel affirmed, I’d be doing you and your relationship a disservice. I’d rather name the prejudice kindly than reinforce it silently. ### But Here’s What I Think Is Really Happening I don’t think you’re a bigot. I think you’re a parent who has seen people hurt, who feels protective, and who is now caught between a cultural whisper you’ve heard all your life and a daughter you love who is choosing something that feels risky to you. That’s an incredibly tough spot. You’re not alone in that tension. Many parents face it. And the reason I’m pushing back isn’t to win a debate or deny your experiences—it’s because if you bring this assumption into a conversation with your daughter, it will almost certainly backfire. She’ll hear it as “my parent thinks my partner is inherently untrustworthy because of who they are,” and that will damage your relationship, possibly for years. You could lose her confidence entirely. ### So What Can You Do? Instead of settling the debate about “most people” and statistics, let’s zoom in on what you can actually control: your connection with your daughter. You can absolutely express general care: “I just want you to be with someone who treats you well, who’s honest, and who’s there for the long haul. Tell me what makes you feel safe and loved with this person.” That’s not denial; it’s focus. It lets you keep your protective instincts without making her partner’s orientation the target. And if her partner ever does hurt her, she’ll come to you because she knows you trusted her enough to give them a fair chance, not because you were waiting to say “I told you so.” I’m not the only one who knows bisexuality doesn’t cause cheating—I’m just the one saying it out loud to you right now, because your daughter needs you to hear it more than she needs a cultural consensus.
