00:13:58 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a powerful analogy, and I can see exactly why it feels like it captures something true. You're describing a world where commitment is partly a function of scarcity — where having fewer options makes it easier to be all-in, and having more options naturally loosens the grip. I think that's a genuinely intuitive way to think about choices, and in many areas of life, it holds up. We probably all know someone who kept job-hopping or second-guessing because they had too many alternatives. So let me honor the analogy before I gently suggest where it might break down. ### The Job Offer Model vs. The Love Model A job is a transaction. You evaluate the offer, compare it to others, and make a calculated decision based on salary, location, growth, and fit. Even after you accept, you might keep an eye on the market. The relationship is fundamentally conditional: you stay as long as it serves your interests. So yes, someone with multiple offers might feel less "locked in" — because in a transactional model, commitment is a function of limited alternatives. But a loving, committed relationship isn't (or shouldn't be) a transaction. It's a covenant. The goal isn't to pick the best available option from a pool and then stay only because nothing better came along. It's to build something together, to give your word, to invest your whole self. In that model, the presence of other hypothetical options becomes irrelevant — not because they don't exist, but because you've already made your choice. You're not "locked in" by scarcity; you're freely, fully given. ### The Real Question Isn't "How Many Options?" — It's "What Kind of Person Are They?" Imagine two people: - Person A has only ever been attracted to one gender. They meet their partner, commit, and stay. But deep down, they stay partly because they don't think they could find anyone better. - Person B is bisexual. They could theoretically be attracted to a wider range of people. But they meet their partner, fall deeply in love, and commit with their whole heart — not because they lack options, but because they genuinely, fully choose this one person. Who is more "faithful"? I'd argue Person B. Because their commitment isn't a product of limited circumstances. It's a product of full-hearted, willful, daily choosing. The "more options" didn't weaken their commitment — it revealed its strength. They aren't staying because the door is closed; they're staying because they've chosen not to walk through it, even though it's wide open. ### The Hidden Assumption in the Job Analogy The analogy assumes that people are constantly evaluating their relationships against a field of alternatives — that we're all just keeping one eye on the door. That's a cynical view of human nature, and unfortunately, it describes some relationships. But it's not the only way people operate. Many people, across all orientations, genuinely stop "looking around" once they've committed. Not because they've been blinded, but because their love has reoriented their desires. Other people stop being opportunities and just become... people. The buffet disappears because you're not hungry for anything else. That's not naivety. It's how real intimacy works. When you truly love someone, the question "what else is out there?" stops being interesting. It's not that you're suppressing it — it's that it fades. ### So Yes, Options Could Increase Temptation — If the Person Is Transactional If someone views relationships like jobs — always optimizing, always comparing — then more options might indeed make them less "locked in." But that's a character flaw, not an orientation. A straight person with a transactional view of relationships will also keep looking around, even if their options are limited to one gender. A bisexual person with a covenant view of love will not. The variable that matters isn't the number of genders in play. It's whether the person sees commitment as a cage or a choice. ### Bringing It Back to Your Daughter This is where I'd gently ask: does her partner seem like a transactional person? Someone always calculating their next move, never fully present, keeping their options open? Or do they seem like someone who has chosen her with their whole self, who shows up fully, who acts like the search is over? If it's the latter, then the "job offer" analogy simply doesn't describe them. And it would be unfair to project it onto them just because they're bisexual. Their commitment might actually be more meaningful precisely because they had more abstract options and still chose her. I know this doesn't undo the analogy for you entirely. But I hope it at least opens a crack in the door — the possibility that love, when it's real, doesn't operate by the logic of scarcity. It operates by the logic of gift.
